Tile Engine Design Document
Daniel Jimenez
18-11-2017 (revised 26-07-2026 to match the implementation)

This document details the design of a general purpose and lightweight tile
engine. The target architecture is the libretro API, with priority for
desktop (RetroArch), 3DS, and eventually Wii/PSP compatibility.

The system is intended for simplicity of design and high performance. It is
written from a low-level perspective in C, with minimal dependencies
(libxmp for module music, optional at build time). Graphics are prepared
with the bmptogfx utility from indexed BMP tilesets plus palette files.

Specifications (as implemented)

All color is 16 bit ARGB (1,5,5,5). A set alpha bit marks a color as
semitransparent: the renderer blends it 50/50 with whatever lies behind it.

The rendering system consists of two background layers and two sprite
layers, composited in software back to front: BG1, BG0, SP0, SP1. The
viewport is 320x240 (400x240 on 3DS; other sizes are future work).

Background layers (BG0 and BG1)
 - 512x512 pixels: 32x32 tilemap of 16x16 tiles. The power-of-two size
   makes all wraparound arithmetic free.
 - Each layer has 4 sub-palettes of 16 colors (64 colors per layer);
   each tilemap entry selects its sub-palette with 2 bits.
 - Palette index 0 is transparent on BG0 (BG1 shows through) and is the
   backdrop color on BG1.
 - Per-scanline x/y offsets per layer (parallax, raster effects).
 - Tilemap entry: disable bit, v-flip/h-flip bits (reserved, background
   flips not implemented yet), 2-bit sub-palette, 10-bit tile index.
 - Tilemap entries can be rewritten at runtime for block animation.

Sprite layers
 - SP0 "full sprites": 32 sprites of 16x16, 8 palettes of 16 colors.
 - SP1 "half sprites": 128 sprites of 8x8, 8 palettes of 16 colors
   (used for bullets, score and HUD text; tiles indexed by ASCII code).
 - Palette index 0 is transparent on both sprite layers.
 - All graphics are 4 bits per pixel. (The 3bpp half-sprite format from
   the original document was never implemented and has been dropped;
   it remains a possible memory optimization for PSP-class targets.)
 - OAM transforms per sprite: h-flip, v-flip, 90-degree rotation and
   double size. rotation+h_flip+v_flip composes to a -90 rotation.
   H-flip and rotation index pre-computed transformed tilesets built at
   load time (memory for speed); v-flip and double size are computed at
   render time (cpu).
 - OAM slots are allocated from a free list; allocation and deletion
   are O(1).

GFX file format (as written by bmptogfx)

 File header "GFX\n"  - 4 bytes
 Pixel bitdepth       - 1 byte (4; only 4bpp is supported)
 Palette qty          - 1 byte
 Palette data         - qty x 16 colors x 2 bytes, big-endian ARGB1555
 Tile size            - 1 byte (16 or 8)
 Tile qty             - 2 bytes, big-endian
 Tile data            - qty x (size x size / 2) bytes, 4bpp packed,
                        leftmost pixel in the high nibble

gfxtool (tools/gfxtool.c) builds and inspects assets:
 gfxtool build <manifest> <out.gfx>
   Manifests (assets/*.mfst) declare type (background/fullsprite/
   halfsprite), tileset images (PNG or BMP) and palettes (16-color
   images, first 16 raster pixels; alpha=i,j marks semitransparent
   colors). 8-bit indexed BMPs contribute their pixel indices directly;
   other images are matched RGB-exact against the palette and fail
   loudly on unknown colors.
 gfxtool dump <in.gfx> <out.png>
   Tileset contact sheet for inspection.
 gfxtool import-scene <image> <palette> <out.gfx> <out.map>
   Slices a 512x512 scene image into tiles, deduplicates identical
   tiles, and emits tileset + tilemap.

MAP file format (tilemaps, loaded with read_map_data):
 File header "MAP\n"  - 4 bytes
 Width, height        - 1 byte each (32x32)
 Entries              - w x h x 2 bytes, big-endian tilemap words

Scenes: the boot menu selects a scene; each scene is a bg0 tileset
(.gfx) plus a tilemap (.map, or procedural). Scene 2's art is generated
by tools/gen_scene2.py.

Save states

Full game, OAM and background state (about 15 KB), versioned and
validated with a magic number. The game loop is deterministic, so
RetroArch rewind and netplay-style replay work from these states.
Save states are not endian-portable, and the music position is not
saved (the module keeps playing through a load).

ROADMAP TO 0.1

 [x] Full sprites with h/v flip, rotation and double size
 [x] Half sprites for bullets and HUD
 [x] Controls and interaction
 [x] Collisions
 [x] Module music (libxmp); sound effects pending
 [x] Save states
 [ ] Dedicated art for ships, explosions and bullets
 [ ] Sound effects for bullets and hits
 [ ] One level and boss
